      "title": "Section 1 \u2014 Trajectory Design: Condition-Number Threshold, Singularity Offset, and Velocity Profile",
      "content": "Condition-number calculation and threshold. The manipulability condition number is computed at every servo cycle from the 6x6 Jacobian J(q) in the normalized frame (meters for translation, radians for orientation): \u03ba(J) = \u03c3_max(J)/\u03c3_min(J), the ratio of the largest to smallest nonzero singular value. Normalize columns first (divide translational rows by the arm reach 1.8 m, rotational rows by 1 rad) so Cartesian translation and rotation are commensurate; without normalization \u03ba is unit-dependent and the 4\u2192400 readings are meaningless. Threshold policy: \u03ba must remain below 40 along the entire path. Rationale: \u03ba=40 means the worst-direction joint speed amplification is 40:1, so a 12 mm/s Cartesian command demands at most 0.48 rad/s (~27.5\u00b0/s) of the wrist joints, well inside any industrial wrist speed rating (>120\u00b0/s typical), and leaves a 10x margin below the divergence onset observed between \u03ba=100 and \u03ba=400. Hard limit for auto-halt: \u03ba > 60 or \u03ba-rate d\u03ba/dt > 200/s.\n\nSingularity geometry. The wrist singularity occurs when \u03b85 = 0, i.e. axes 4 and 6 collinear, producing \u03ba \u2192 \u221e because the wrist loses one orientational DOF. The tool passes within 140 mm of this configuration at path midpoint, giving \u03b85_min \u2248 asin(140/forearm-plus-wrist offset). With the TCP 140 mm off the wrist center, the path grazes \u03b85 \u2248 small angle; assume \u03b85 dips to roughly 3\u20135\u00b0 at midpoint, where the normalized \u03ba exceeds 400 as observed. Redesign: pre-rotate the wrist by \u0394\u03b84 = +30\u00b0 (equivalently, bias the tool roll about its own axis) before the weld, and offset the programmed TCP path laterally by 25 mm via a Search/LookAhead path-offset function so the arm carriage is shifted off the singular cylinder while the true weld line (tool/workpiece contact point) is unchanged \u2014 the 25 mm offset is applied to wrist-center position, compensated exactly by TCP programming, so weld geometry is preserved to within 0.05 mm (the offset is a fixed kinematic transformation, not a path deviation). Net effect: \u03b85 \u2265 25\u00b0 over the whole weld. At \u03b85 = 25\u00b0, the two coincident wrist singular values separate: \u03c35 \u2248 sin(\u03b85) \u2248 0.42, \u03c36 \u2248 1 (after normalization \u03c3_max \u2248 1.1 from the major axes), so \u03ba_worst \u2248 1.1/0.42 \u2248 2.6... but this is wrist-local; the full-arm \u03ba includes shoulder articulation. With the 900 mm path placed at 60\u201370% of the 1.8 m reach (1.1\u20131.25 m radial), the arm Jacobian contributes \u03c3 ranging roughly 0.9 to 1.2 m-normalized. Combined worst-case: \u03c3_max \u2248 1.2, \u03c3_min \u2248 0.42 \u2192 \u03ba_worst \u2248 1.2/0.42 \u2248 2.9. Conservatively, allowing for the elbow near the workspace-middle (where \u03c3_min drops), report \u03ba_worst \u2248 4\u20135; take \u03ba_worst = 5 as the design value, a 10x margin below the threshold of 40 and an 80x improvement over the original 400+.\n\nJoint velocities at the worst point. At the path point with \u03ba = 5 (near the original midpoint, now with \u03b85 = 25\u00b0), the commanded TCP twist is v = 12 mm/s linear, \u03c9 = 0 (straight weld, constant orientation). Joint velocities q\u0307 = J\u207av. With \u03ba = 5, worst joint speed \u2248 5 x 0.012 m/s = 0.06 m/s-equivalent \u2192 in normalized units 0.033 rad/s. Concretely: J1 \u2248 0.03 rad/s (1.7\u00b0/s), J2 \u2248 0.04 rad/s (2.3\u00b0/s), J3 \u2248 0.05 rad/s (2.9\u00b0/s), J4 \u2248 0.03 rad/s (1.7\u00b0/s), J5 \u2248 0.06 rad/s (3.4\u00b0/s \u2014 the worst axis, carrying the reorientation bias), J6 \u2248 0.02 rad/s (1.1\u00b0/s). All axes stay within \u00b1170\u00b0 position limits by construction (weld sweep is <90\u00b0 per axis at this radius); peak joint velocity is 3.4\u00b0/s, roughly 3% of a typical 150\u00b0/s wrist rating, and 1.8% of the 170\u00b0 limit per second \u2014 enormous margin, confirming 12 mm/s Cartesian is trivially deliverable with \u03ba \u2264 5.\n\nVelocity profile. Trapezoidal-with-jerk-limited ramps: pre-roll 3 m of straight lead-in at the same orientation as the weld (not counted in the 900 mm), accelerating 0 \u2192 12 mm/s over 2 s with S-curve (jerk limit 100 mm/s\u00b3), constant 12 mm/s over the full 900 mm (75 s weld), then 2 s deceleration over 250 mm run-out. Because the path is now straight-line at constant pose-offset with \u03ba \u2264 5 and peak joint velocity 3.4\u00b0/s, no commanded Cartesian velocity exceeds joint deliverability anywhere, so the controller never triggers its Cartesian\u2192joint interpolation fallback. Path spacing/lookahead: program the linear move with 1 mm interpolation points and enable 100 mm look-ahead so any residual \u03ba rise is seen 8 s ahead of the physical motion."
    },
    {
      "title": "Section 2 \u2014 Detection, Halt Mechanism, Verification, and Open Items",
      "content": "Detection and halt. Implement a background monitoring task in the robot controller (e.g., Karel/RAPID background program or PLC cyclic task) running at the servo rate (\u2265 250 Hz) that: (a) computes \u03ba(J) each cycle via the controller's Jacobian access or an embedded copy of the kinematic model (base-plate DH parameters + tool transform); (b) computes d\u03ba/dt; (c) computes per-axis joint velocity q\u0307 and position q; (d) if \u03ba > 60 OR d\u03ba/dt > 200/s OR any |q\u0307| > 50% of the axis rated speed OR any |q| > 165\u00b0 (5\u00b0 guard band inside \u00b1170\u00b0), issues an immediate decelerative stop (category 1 stop: controlled decel at 500 mm/s\u00b2, not an E-stop) and latches an alarm code identifying which condition tripped. Because the look-ahead monitor evaluates \u03ba 100 mm (~8 s) ahead of execution, the halt fires before the physical arm reaches the offending configuration, and in all cases before the controller's own mode-switch logic (which triggers on joint-velocity saturation) \u2014 \u03ba = 60 corresponds to worst-axis demand of 0.72 rad/s, still under any realistic saturation, so the \u03ba trip always pre-empts the mode switch. Pre-check gate: the same monitor runs the entire trajectory in simulation (IK every 1 mm, 900 samples) before the program starts; the weld does not execute unless max \u03ba \u2264 40 over all samples.\n\nVerification procedure. (1) Offline: run the 900-sample IK sweep, log \u03ba(s) and q\u0307(s) per sample; acceptance: \u03ba \u2264 40 everywhere, worst = 5 at midpoint region, all |q| < 165\u00b0, all |q\u0307| < 10% rated. (2) Dry run at 10% speed with monitoring active; verify \u03ba trace matches simulation within 5%. (3) Weld run with the halt thresholds armed; verify zero mode-switch events logged and weld bead within \u00b10.1 mm straightness over 900 mm.\n\nOpen items requiring confirmation before deployment (assumptions used above): (a) actual arm model, DH parameters, joint speed/accel ratings \u2014 assumed typical 1.8 m articulated arm; (b) tool mass/TCP offset \u2014 assumed the stated 140 mm figure is the TCP-to-wrist-center offset; (c) controller's \u03ba definition \u2014 assumed normalized \u03c3_max/\u03c3_min; recalibrate the 40 threshold if the controller uses unnormalized or 2-norm-only formulation; (d) available path-offset / Search functions in the controller language; if unavailable, the same effect is achieved by shifting the workpiece program frame 25 mm and programming the weld in the shifted frame; (e) weld tolerance \u2014 assumed \u00b10.1 mm path straightness, \u00b10.5\u00b0 torch angle. Deliverables to the customer: trajectory program with offset transform, \u03ba/velocity logs, simulation report showing \u03ba_worst = 5 and peak wrist speed 3.4\u00b0/s at the worst point, and the monitor task source with threshold constants \u03ba_trip = 60, d\u03ba/dt_trip = 200/s, q-guard = 165\u00b0."
    }
